      "source": [
        "Problem Statement Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "Diabetes is a major global public health challenge, with rising prevalence and significant long-term health and economic consequences. Early identification of individuals at high risk of developing diabetes is therefore critical to enable timely clinical intervention, improve patient outcomes, and reduce healthcare costs. Machine Learning (ML) techniques offer the potential to support such early detection by identifying complex patterns in medical and lifestyle data that may not be apparent through traditional statistical methods.\n",
        "\n",
        "This project aims to develop and evaluate predictive ML models to classify whether an individual is likely to be diagnosed with diabetes based on a set of diagnostic, demographic, and behavioural features. The analysis uses a modified version of the Pima Indians Diabetes dataset provided by the university, which contains medical measurements from women aged 21 years and above of Pima Indian heritage. In addition to standard clinical variables such as glucose levels, blood pressure, body mass index (BMI), and age, the dataset includes synthetic features representing physical activity levels and family history to simulate real-world healthcare data complexity.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dataset intentionally incorporates common data-quality challenges, including missing or invalid values, duplicate records, and mild outliers, requiring careful exploratory analysis and preprocessing prior to model development. Beyond technical performance, the project also addresses the ethical dimensions of predictive healthcare modelling, particularly issues related to demographic bias, fairness, generalisability, and the real-world consequences of incorrect predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "By applying the full ML lifecycle—from exploratory data analysis and data preparation to model training, evaluation, and ethical reflection—this project seeks to assess both the effectiveness and limitations of ML-based diabetes prediction, while highlighting the responsibilities associated with deploying such models in healthcare settings."
      ],
